# IPC contract

Renderer access uses only `window.flowpilot`; Node, Electron, filesystem, and
SQLite are not exposed. The API is defined by `FlowPilotApi` in `contracts.ts`,
with `Result<T>` and `ErrorCode` in `ipc-result.ts`.

- `snapshot.load()` (`snapshot.load`): empty strict request; returns
  an `IpcSnapshot`.
- `commands.apply()` (`commands.apply`): strict
  `{ commands, expectedRevision }`; returns the updated `IpcSnapshot`.
- `settings.get()` (`settings.get`): empty strict request; returns settings.
- `settings.set()` (`settings.set`): strict
  `{ key, value, expectedRevision }`; returns the updated `IpcSnapshot`.
- `app.info()` (`app.info`): empty strict request; returns `{ name, version }`.

Every reply is `{ ok: true, value }` or `{ ok: false, error }`. Error codes:
`StaleRevision`, `ValidationFailed`, `UndoConflict`, `NotFound`, and
`InternalError`. Schemas reject unknown fields. Settings writes use the same
expected-revision transaction as commands. No filesystem paths cross the
bridge; main resolves `<profile>/flowpilot.db` and owns the single writer.
`IpcSnapshot` carries dates and times as ISO strings for safe IPC serialization.
AI proposals use a separate strict command contract in `ai-commands.ts`.
`task.classify`, `rule.import`, `rule.activate`, and `task.setAiRank` are internal
main-to-storage commands and are intentionally absent from `ipcCommandSchema`.
The proposal validator checks snapshot IDs and revision/rule fingerprints before
returning commands tagged with `source: 'ai'`; it does not call a provider or
apply writes itself.
AI `task.update` accepts only user-editable fields. AI `task.create` uses a
separate strict input shape; conversion derives status from the target column,
assigns the next add order, and initializes comments and attachments without
accepting system fields from the proposal.

Test variables `FLOWPILOT_TEST`, `FLOWPILOT_TEST_NOW`, and
`FLOWPILOT_TEST_PROFILE` are honored only for unpackaged applications with
`FLOWPILOT_TEST=1`. Packaged apps always use system time and Electron's
user-data directory. The injected `Clock` makes tests deterministic.
